---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="From the Field" description="Video and field notes from Filthy Rich Soil Co. — how we think about soil, plants, and the craft of land stewardship in Chester County, PA.">

<style is:global>
  .media-hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: 60vh;
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    padding: 80px 5% 80px;
    background-image: url('https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1441974231531-c6227db76b6e?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1800&q=80');
    background-size: cover;
    background-position: center;
    background-attachment: fixed;
  }
  .media-hero::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    background: linear-gradient(to top, rgba(1,20,10,0.92) 0%, rgba(1,20,10,0.45) 60%, transparent 100%);
  }
  .media-hero .hero-content { position: relative; z-index: 1; max-width: 600px; }
  .media-hero h1 {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
    font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 5.5vw, 5rem);
    font-weight: 900; font-style: italic;
    color: #FAF7F0; line-height: 1.05; margin-bottom: 20px;
  }
  .media-hero p {
    font-size: 1.1rem; font-weight: 300;
    color: rgba(250,247,240,0.75); max-width: 480px; line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .media-video-section {
    background: #080e08;
    padding: 80px 5% 100px;
  }
  .media-video-inner { max-width: 1200px; margin: 0 auto; }

  .video-grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); gap: 32px; }
  .video-card {
    background: rgba(1,30,16,0.55);
    border: 1px solid rgba(184,134,11,0.15);
    border-radius: 12px; overflow: hidden;
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    transition: transform 0.35s, box-shadow 0.35s, border-color 0.3s;
  }
  .video-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-6px);
    box-shadow: 0 24px 64px rgba(0,0,0,0.5);
    border-color: rgba(184,134,11,0.35);
  }
  .video-thumb {
    height: 260px; background-size: cover; background-position: center;
    position: relative; cursor: pointer; overflow: hidden;
  }
  .play-btn {
    position: absolute; top: 50%; left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%,-50%);
    width: 68px; height: 68px; border-radius: 50%;
    background: rgba(250,247,240,0.92); backdrop-filter: blur(8px);
    display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    transition: transform 0.2s, background 0.2s;
  }
  .play-btn svg { width: 26px; height: 26px; fill: #013220; margin-left: 4px; }
  .video-card:hover .play-btn { transform: translate(-50%,-50%) scale(1.1); background: white; }
  .video-duration {
    position: absolute; bottom: 14px; right: 14px;
    background: rgba(0,0,0,0.65); color: rgba(250,247,240,0.9);
    font-size: 0.7rem; padding: 4px 10px; border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: 'DM Sans', sans-serif; font-weight: 500;
  }
  .video-body { padding: 26px 28px 32px; }
  .video-body h3 {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
    font-size: 1.2rem; color: #FAF7F0; margin-bottom: 10px; line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .video-body p { font-size: 0.9rem; color: rgba(250,247,240,0.6); line-height: 1.75; }

  .notes-section {
    background: #0a140a;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(184,134,11,0.12);
    padding: 80px 5% 100px;
  }
  .notes-inner { max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; }

  .note-item {
    padding: 44px 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(184,134,11,0.12);
  }
  .note-item:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
  .note-date {
    font-size: 0.65rem; font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 2.5px;
    color: #B8860B; margin-bottom: 10px; display: block;
  }
  .note-item h3 {
    font-family: 'Playfair Display', serif;
    font-size: 1.45rem; color: #FAF7F0; margin-bottom: 18px; line-height: 1.3;
  }
  .note-item p {
    font-size: 0.98rem; color: rgba(250,247,240,0.62); line-height: 1.9;
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }
  .note-item p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .video-grid { grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .media-hero { background-attachment: scroll; }
  }
</style>

<section class="media-hero">
  <div class="hero-content">
    <h1>From the<br>Field</h1>
    <p>Video from the properties we work on, and notes on how we think about soil, plants, and the craft.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── Video / channel ─────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="media-video-section">
  <div class="media-video-inner" style="max-width:800px;">
    <span class="section-eyebrow reveal">Video</span>
    <h2 class="section-title reveal" style="margin-bottom:24px">The work, on camera</h2>
    <p class="reveal" style="font-size:1rem;color:rgba(250,247,240,0.72);line-height:1.85;margin-bottom:32px;">We publish what we're doing as we do it — pruning decisions, plantings going in, the reasoning behind the calls we make on a property. Not highlight reels. The honest work, including the parts that take longer than we'd like.</p>
    <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@FilthyRichSoilCo" class="btn-primary reveal" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube</a>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── Field Notes ─────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="notes-section">
  <div class="notes-inner">
    <span class="section-eyebrow reveal">Field Notes</span>
    <h2 class="section-title reveal" style="margin-bottom:52px">How we think about the work</h2>

    <div class="note-item reveal">
      <span class="note-date">September 2026</span>
      <h3>Why we test soil in the fall — and why we charge for it</h3>
      <p>Most people think about their soil in April, when they can see what's wrong. By then the window for doing much about it has mostly closed. The useful time to sample is October and November: the season's growth is done, the soil has been through a full year of whatever was done to it, and there are five months ahead to plan amendments and get them in the ground before roots start demanding resources.</p>
      <p>That's why our first step with any property is a fall assessment — multi-point sampling, a full lab panel, and a written account of what's happening and why. We charge for it, and the fee is credited if we go on to work together. The reason is simple: a free estimate is a sales visit. A paid assessment is a diagnosis. You should know which one you're getting.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="note-item reveal">
      <span class="note-date">September 2026</span>
      <h3>What compacted soil is actually costing you</h3>
      <p>Compaction gets treated as a surface problem. The grass looks thin, the beds look rough, so the answer is to dethatch, aerate, overseed, and try again next spring. Same result.</p>
      <p>It isn't a surface problem. When soil particles get pressed together — from foot traffic, equipment, heavy clay, or years of conventional lawn care — the pore space disappears. And pore space is where everything happens: water infiltration, root extension, oxygen exchange, microbial activity. Take it away and the soil doesn't just perform poorly. It stops functioning as soil at all. What's left is a growing medium that can't move water, can't support deep roots, and can't sustain the biology that makes nutrients available. The grass survives on what's pushed into it from above. Remove the inputs and it fails — which is why it always seems to need more.</p>
      <p>The fix isn't aggressive aeration followed by more seed. It's restoring structure: biological amendments that rebuild aggregation, organic matter that creates pore space over time, and a stop to the practices that caused it. It takes a season or two to see fully. But soil that comes out the other side doesn't need constant management. It runs itself.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="note-item reveal">
      <span class="note-date">September 2026</span>
      <h3>On invasive species — what removal actually requires</h3>
      <p>Pulling invasives is one of the most common things landowners ask for, and it's usually approached wrong. A stand of Japanese knotweed or English ivy gets ripped out and the problem is considered solved. Three months later it's back, sometimes worse.</p>
      <p>Removal without replacement is just an opportunity. You've cleared the space, reduced competition, and improved growing conditions — for whatever comes back first. If that's native plants you've intentionally established, good. If it's more of the same invasive, or a different one that moves in opportunistically, you've done a lot of work for nothing.</p>
      <p>Effective invasive management treats the removal and the replacement as one project. You clear, you plant, and you monitor. Native ground covers, shrubs, and perennials go in fast — dense enough to hold the space. On mature stands of woody invasives, that means multiple removal sessions over two or three years as the root system exhausts itself. It's slower than people want. But cutting knotweed once and hoping is treating a symptom and ignoring the condition. The biology doesn't care about your timeline.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="note-item reveal">
      <span class="note-date">September 2026</span>
      <h3>Why we don't do one-time services</h3>
      <p>We'll be asked for it. A one-time cleanup, a fall cutback, a mulch refresh. It feels like a contained thing, and there are plenty of landscapers who will show up, do it, and move on.</p>
      <p>We don't work that way, and it's worth explaining why. A property cleaned up in October and left alone until spring accumulates six months of deferred decisions — what to cut back and what to leave standing for overwintering insects, what to mulch and how deep, which plants are struggling and which are establishing. None of those decisions are obvious, and all of them matter.</p>
      <p>When we take on a property, we're not doing tasks. We're building knowledge — how the soil drains after a wet week, where the deer pressure is heaviest, which plantings are thriving. That knowledge compounds. A one-time service starts from zero every time and optimizes for what looks right on the day. We'd rather pass and let you find someone set up for that.</p>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

</Base>
